<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Khorasan</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Khurasan, Khurasani</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Khorasan is a historical region of north-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a> and adjoining parts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-asia">Central Asia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afghanistan">Afghanistan</a>, containing cities such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nishapur">Nishapur</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/merv">Merv</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herat">Herat</a>. It was the launching ground of the Abbasid revolution against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/umayyad-caliphate">Umayyad Caliphate</a> and the base from which the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seljuk-empire">Seljuks</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nader-shah">Nader Shah</a>'s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afsharid-empire">Afsharid Empire</a> rose. Its frontier position made it contested ground for the Safavid and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timurid-empire">Timurid Empires</a> as well.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/khorasan.jpg" alt="Khorasan" width="320" height="324" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Hairy Dude · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Khorasan-Transoxiana-Khwarazm_nopaths.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The revolution was driven by widespread discontent with Umayyad Arab exclusivism, a policy that marginalised non-Arab Muslims, particularly Persian converts known as mawali, and was catalysed by the charismatic generalship of Abu Muslim, a commander of obscure but probably Persian origin operating from <b>Khorasan</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/abbasid-caliphate">Abbasid Caliphate · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The Afsharid Empire was founded in 1736 by Nader Shah, one of history's most remarkable military commanders, who had risen from a modest background in the Turkic Afshar tribe of <b>Khorasan</b> to become the most powerful man in Iran before formally seizing the throne.</blockquote><a href="/empire/afsharid-empire">Afsharid Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>In 800 the Abbasid caliph Harun al-Rashid appointed Ibrahim I ibn al-Aghlab, the son of a <b>Khurasani</b> Arab commander of the Banu Tamim tribe, hereditary emir of Ifriqiya, in answer to the anarchy that had followed the fall of the Muhallabids in that province.</blockquote><a href="/empire/aghlabid-emirate">Aghlabid emirate · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The Durrani Empire (1747–1823) was built by Ahmad Shah Durrani from the wreck of Nader Shah's Persia into a Pashtun state reaching from <b>Khorasan</b> to the Punjab, victorious over the Marathas at Panipat in 1761 but unable to hold down the Sikhs.</blockquote><a href="/empire/durrani-empire">Durrani Empire · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>Trade routes across the Iranian plateau, including the great road later known as the <b>Khorasan</b> route, linked Media with Assyrian, Babylonian and later Anatolian markets, carrying metals, textiles and livestock.</blockquote><a href="/empire/median-empire">Median Empire · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>After the war Britain sought a protectorate through the Anglo-Persian Agreement of 1919, which the Majlis never ratified, while separatist and revolutionary movements arose in Gilan, Azerbaijan and <b>Khorasan</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/qajar-iran">Qajar Iran · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li></ul></section>
